WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.230
Imagine a person who always makes the right
financial decisions.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.160
Let’s call her “Penny.”

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.740
Penny approaches every money problem with
perfect logic and reason.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.300
She never gets emotional or impulsive and
always knows what’s in her own best interests.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.820
You’re probably thinking that Penny sounds
imaginary (and kind of obnoxious).

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.880
But for over a hundred years, most economists
believed that the world was made up entirely

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:29.880
of Pennys.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.840
That you and I and everyone you know always
made the best decisions to maximize our happiness.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.760
As crazy as that sounds, that was the conventional
wisdom... until a small group of economists

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.420
and psychologists started to question whether
Penny actually existed at all.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:51.260
One of them, Richard Thaler, won the Nobel
Prize last year for proving that not only

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.600
do humans make financial mistakes, they make
predictable mistakes.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.670
He joked about calling his research “Dumb
Stuff People Do,” but today it’s called

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.079
behavioral economics, and it has changed public
policy across the world.

00:01:05.079 --> 00:01:10.159
Thaler showed that humans can’t quite remove
their emotions from the decision-making process,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.860
but being able to predict these money mistakes
might help YOU avoid them.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:23.340
[MUSIC]

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.079
Let’s say you’re helping your mom clean
out the garage and you find a pack of Pokemon

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:31.399
cards that you must’ve bought when you were
a kid and forgot to open.

00:01:31.399 --> 00:01:36.509
So you go through them and HOLY COW there’s
a first edition Charizard in mint condition!

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:42.509
Even though you could easily get $3,000 on
ebay for it, you decide to buy a frame and

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:44.819
keep it on a shelf in your apartment.

00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:49.829
Okay, let’s rewind and consider a different
scenario: Instead of helping your mom clean

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:52.700
the garage, you decide to go to the comic
book store.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:58.639
There in a glass case you see a first edition
Charizard card priced at $3,000.

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:02.439
Even though you used to love Pokemon, you’d
never dream of shelling out that kind of dough

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:05.380
for a playing card, and you leave the store.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.399
This may seem like understandable behavior,
but it’s completely irrational.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:14.560
In the first scenario, you’re deciding that
a Charizard card is worth giving up $3,000

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.430
for, but in the second scenario, you’re
deciding a Charizard card is not worth giving

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.790
up $3,000 for.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:26.700
To a perfectly rational being like Penny,
whether or not you already own the card should

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.790
be irrelevant when judging its value, but
clearly to humans like you and me, it’s

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.069
very relevant.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:39.040
Thaler called this the “endowment effect,”
our tendency to assign more value to the things

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.840
we already own than the things we could own.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.400
Any time you refuse to sell something for
an amount that’s more than you’d pay for

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.099
it, you are experiencing the endowment effect.

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:52.909
Ugh, this movie is awful!

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:54.030
I know.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.349
Let’s watch something else.

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:56.349
We can’t.

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:57.349
Why not?

00:02:57.349 --> 00:02:59.299
Because I paid 6 bucks to rent this movie!

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:00.820
We can’t just throw that money away!

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.820
Ugh!

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.820
You burned the popcorn!

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.490
Well, we have to finish it.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.109
That bag cost 89¢.

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:12.050
Have you ever watched a movie you hated all
the way to the end or finished a meal you

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.720
weren’t enjoying, just because you paid
for it and you wanted to get “your money’s

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.720
worth”?

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.239
If so, you have fallen victim to the SUNK
COST FALLACY.

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:25.620
It’s not like sitting through the Emoji
Movie will magically refund the rental price--that

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.030
money is sunk, and it’s not coming back,
so why put yourself through the extra pain?

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.469
Most of us keep an emotional balance sheet
in our head, which is less concerned with

00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:39.819
actual gains and losses than the feeling of
gains and losses.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.579
Even though watching the movie makes you less
happy, at least if you sit through it you

00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:47.439
don’t have to count that $6 as a loss in
your mental checkbook.

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:53.180
Our fear of sunk costs is so great that businesses
can use it to squeeze even more money out

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.180
of us.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.409
Many retailers sell "memberships" that include perks like discounts and free shipping.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:04.879
They know some many people will buy extra
stuff they don’t really need, just to make

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:07.909
sure they get “their money’s worth”.

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:11.779
Imagine that you’re shopping for some headphones
and you’re about to buy some for $15, when

00:04:11.779 --> 00:04:16.120
you find out that the store down the street
is selling the exact same pair for $10.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.019
It’s just a ten minute walk.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.019
Would you do it?

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.450
Now, same scenario, but this time you’re
shopping for a laptop.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.150
The first store has the model you want for
$675.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.830
The second store $670.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.680
Now do you take the walk?

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.150
Many people will answer these two questions
differently, even though they are essentially

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.080
the same question: Is it worth $5 to take
a 10-minute walk?

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.200
All other context should be irrelevant.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.610
Yet people are more likely to say yes to the
first scenario, because it feels like you’re

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.860
getting a better deal.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:51.430
This is called transaction utility, the amount
of mental pleasure or pain we get from feeling

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:56.800
like we paid less or more than something’s
really worth, and it’s often totally disconnected

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.090
from the happiness you get from the thing
itself.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:05.530
Stores have been exploiting transaction utility
from time immemorial, like the notoriously

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:11.180
inflated “manufacturer’s suggested retail
price,” which makes it look like every item

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.700
is always on sale.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:17.590
And some shoppers are so addicted to transaction
utility that they’ll fill their houses with

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.850
stuff they don’t need and will never use
just to chase that bargain high.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:27.080
Of course, it’s always important to shop
around to find the best deal, but remember

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.569
that when considering any purchase, the only
thing that matters is what it’s worth to

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:32.569
you.

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:36.930
If you won $100 on a lottery scratcher, what
would you do with the money?

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.260
Buy an expensive pair of shoes?

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.510
Go out for a fancy dinner?

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:44.729
Most people say that they’re more likely
to spend unexpected income on something indulgent

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:47.960
or frivolous, because hey, it’s not like
I worked for that money.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:48.960
It was free!

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.230
Thaler called this kind of thinking mental
accounting, which means separating money into

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.419
imaginary categories in your mind.

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:02.979
Mental accounting violates the rule that money
is fungible--that is, totally interchangeable.

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:07.550
Once you own a dollar, it’s the same as
any other and shouldn’t be treated differently

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.750
just because of where it came from.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:15.550
For Penny, that pair of shoes is either worth
giving up 100 dollars, or it’s not.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:20.330
Just because some money unexpectedly showed
up doesn’t make the shoes worth more.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.419
Mental accounting can be helpful in making
monthly budgets and sticking to them, but

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:27.440
even then it can lead us down irrational roads.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.569
One study focused on how consumers reacted
to a drop in gas prices from 4 to 2 dollars

00:06:32.569 --> 00:06:33.740
a gallon.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.190
This was at the beginning of the 2008 financial
crisis, so you’d think most families would’ve

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:41.400
had a lot of use for that extra 40 bucks a
week.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.199
Instead, researchers found that people were
surprisingly likely to squander those savings

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:47.530
on a higher grade of gas!

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:52.160
It’s as if in their minds they had budgeted
a certain amount a month to gas, and so it

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.030
had to be spent on gas.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.080
They couldn’t treat the money as fungible.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.409
Mental accounting exists for the same reason
a lot of fallacies do: because our brains

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:06.240
aren’t perfect supercomputers and we use
a lot of mental shortcuts and emotional instincts

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.389
just to get by.

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:11.831
But knowing what those shortcuts are will
make you less likely to rely on them in the

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:12.889
future.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:19.120
You may not ever be as wise as Penny… but
being pennywise is pretty good, too.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.980
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:29.320
If you want to hear more examples of “dumb stuff people do,” check out Richard Thaler’s Misbehaving: The Making of Behavioral Economics.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:38.140
It’s a funny and fascinating history of Thaler’s research, full of surprising studies that will inspire you to think differently about money.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:42.780
[MUSIC]

